Name: lightTheme

Type: property

Syntax: set the lightTheme of <stack> to <themeArray>
Syntax: get the lightTheme of <stack>
Syntax: set the lightTheme to <themeArray>

Summary:
Specifies the colors a <stack> and everything on it are drawn in while the
application is in light mode.

Associations: stack

Introduced: 0.9.16

OS: mac, windows, linux

Platforms: desktop

Example:
local tTheme
put "30,30,46" into tTheme["backgroundColor"]
put "24,24,37" into tTheme["fieldColor"]
put "205,214,244" into tTheme["foregroundColor"]
put "203,166,247" into tTheme["hiliteColor"]
set the lightTheme of this stack to tTheme

Example:
-- go back to the system's colors in light mode
set the lightTheme of this stack to empty

Example:
-- change one color of the theme a stack already has
local tTheme
put the lightTheme of this stack into tTheme
put "255,121,198" into tTheme["hiliteColor"]
set the lightTheme of this stack to tTheme

Value (array):
An <array> of colors, keyed by the name of the color. Each value is a color
reference: an RGB triplet such as "30,30,46", a color name or an HTML-style
color such as "#1E1E2E". The property always reports RGB triplets. A key that
is missing or <empty> leaves that color to the system. Keys other than those
listed below are ignored. The value is <empty> if the stack has no theme for
light mode.

Description:
Use the <lightTheme> property to give a stack its own colors for light mode,
without setting color properties on its objects and without any script that
runs when the appearance changes. The <darkTheme> property does the same for
dark mode. A stack that sets both follows the system from one set of
colors to the other by itself, in the IDE and in a standalone.

The keys of the array are:

- "backgroundColor": the background of the window and of controls
- "foregroundColor": text and lines
- "fieldColor": the background of a bordered field that has no
  <backgroundColor> of its own
- "hiliteColor": selected text, selected list lines and the accent of
  controls (a default button, a progress bar, a checked checkbox, a
  selected tab)
- "hiliteTextColor": text drawn on the "hiliteColor". If it is not set the
  engine uses black or white, whichever can be read on the highlight
- "borderColor": borders
- "linkColor": link text, while the stack's <linkColor> is still the
  engine's default. If it is not set, links keep the default blue, drawn
  lighter on a dark background
- "topColor", "bottomColor" and "shadowColor": the edges of 3D controls and
  the drop shadow of shadowed ones. If "backgroundColor" is set and these
  are not, the engine works them out from the background
- "focusColor": the outline of the focused control

A theme color is used only where nothing else sets that color: an object
whose own color property is set, or that inherits one from its group, card
or stack, keeps it. The theme takes the place of the system's default
colors, so the <effective> color properties of an object report the theme's
colors.

The theme applies to the stack and to its substacks that have no theme of
their own. It is saved in the stack file, so a standalone needs neither the
IDE nor a script to use it. A stack file with a theme can be opened by an
engine that has no support for themes; that engine ignores the theme.

Which of the two themes is in use is decided by the <systemAppearance>, so
it follows the <preferredAppearance> too. While Windows is using a high
contrast theme (the <systemAppearance> is "custom") neither is used and the
system's colors are kept.

Setting the property redraws the stack and sends a <themeChanged> message to
its current card, and to the current card of each of its open substacks.
Controls drawn by the operating system follow the theme where the platform
allows it: on macOS, buttons, menus and tabs are drawn in the light or dark
style that suits the theme's background, and their accent is the theme's
"hiliteColor".

Widgets take part in the same way. A widget that reads its colors while
painting (with `my foreground paint`, `my background color` and so on in
LiveCode Builder) gets the theme's colors, and the widgets that come with
the IDE do so. The Data Grid follows the theme for any color that has not
been set on the grid.

Without an object (`the lightTheme`), the property is the theme of the IDE's own
windows. It applies only to the IDE's stacks, never to the stacks being
worked on, and is not saved by the engine: the IDE sets it from its
Appearance preferences. In a standalone it has no effect.

References: darkTheme (property), themeChanged (message),
systemAppearance (property), preferredAppearance (property),
systemAppearanceChanged (message), backgroundColor (property),
foregroundColor (property), hiliteColor (property), borderColor (property),
focusColor (property), linkColor (property), effective (keyword),
empty (constant), array (glossary), stack (object)
